  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 125 PM MST Thu Nov 12 2026/ \n\n- Meteorological Overview:\n\nStrong upper-level ridging remains in control across Montana today\nand Friday, keeping conditions abnormally warm and dry with\ngeneral breeziness across the region today. By Friday, a potent\nPacific trough begins digging inland along the West Coast,\nincreasing southwesterly flow aloft and tightening the surface\npressure gradient across Southwest Montana. This setup will favor\nincreased winds developing south of I-90, particularly channeled\nwithin north-south oriented valleys. \n\nOn Saturday, the trough tracks eastward across the Great Basin, \nbreaking down the ridge and shifting flow aloft to southerly. This\ntransition initiates a cooling trend that will bring temperatures\nback toward seasonal averages for the remainder of the forecast \nperiod. Moisture transported northward ahead of this wave will be \nheavily limited to Southwest Montana and the higher terrain, \nkeeping most lower elevations dry. With snow levels hovering \naround 7,000 to 8,000 ft, any wintry precipitation will be \nrestricted to the highest mountain peaks primarily from Saturday \ninto Sunday. The fast- moving trough exits toward the Southern \nPlains on Sunday, allowing weak shortwave ridging to briefly \nrebuild by Sunday afternoon with partly sunny, dry conditions and \nseasonable temperatures. \n\nUncertainty increases substantially starting Monday as a deep \nupper-level trough moves inland across Southern California and re-\nestablishes southwesterly flow aloft. While recent guidance shows\na slight uptick in moisture potentially reaching parts of the \narea, centered largely from Tuesday night through Wednesday night,\nmodels remain divided on how this energy evolves. Earlier signals\nfor this system closing off into a cutoff low are becoming less \nlikely; instead, forecast spread now centers on how far south the \ntrough digs and whether it simply progresses eastward or takes on \na negative tilt and propagates northeastward, which would pull \nmore active weather into the region. -JR\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHigh confidence surrounds the unseasonable warmth through Friday \nand the increasing winds in Southwest Montana valleys tomorrow. \n\nConfidence is also high that the Saturday into Sunday system will \nbe low impact, with precipitation probabilities heavily \nconcentrated in far Southwest Montana. Along the Montana/Idaho \nborder, probabilities are 50-60% for exceeding 0.10 inches of \nliquid and 30-40% for exceeding 0.20 inches, dropping to a 20% \nchance for >0.10 inches south of Highway 287. High snow levels \nwill confine accumulations to a dusting from 8,000 ft and up, with\n40-60% chance of >1\u201d restricted solely to the highest peaks of \nthe Centennial Mountains and Blacktail Range. \n\nConfidence drops to low-to-moderate for next week due to ongoing \nmodel spread regarding how far south the incoming trough digs, its\ntilt, and its subsequent track. Currently, the 48-hour \nprobability for receiving 0.25 inches or more of liquid from \nTuesday night through Wednesday night sits at a widespread 20-40%,\nwith higher probabilities favored across Southwest Montana:\n\nScenario 1 (Southern Dig / Progressive Eastward Track): The \nincoming trough digs further south and progresses steadily \neastward across the Great Basin and Central Rockies. This keeps \nthe primary moisture plume and strongest dynamic lift displaced \nsouth of Montana, resulting in near-seasonal temperatures and \nperiodic, light precipitation with minimal winter impacts.\n\nScenario 2 (Negative Tilt / Northeastward Propagation): Rather \nthan progressing straight east, the trough acquires a negative \ntilt and propagates northeastward across the Northern Rockies. \nCapitalizing on the recent uptick in moisture guidance, this \nevolution would bring more widespread precipitation Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday night. In addition to increased precip, this \nscenario would promote further cold air to advect behind the \nsystem bringing the potential for below seasonal norms, though \nconfidence in this track remains lower. -JR",
